# DRAFT (Story 10.7): the King's messenger, the demo's ending. Raphael rewrites every line. The hall opens ~ deliver
# when the player talks to him (scripts/game/royal_messenger.gd); bridge.choose("heard") just before the last line means
# the King's request was delivered: when the box closes, the demo ends (the "to be continued" card, then the main menu).
# Esc before it leaves him waiting: talk again. The King stays distant and the kingdom unnamed; the Demon King is never
# named (narrative-design.md). Game state only through `bridge` (scripts/dialogue/dialogue_bridge.gd).

~ deliver
Royal Messenger: You keep this guild? Good. I was told to find you and no one else.
Royal Messenger: I ride for the King.
Royal Messenger: Your guild's name has reached the capital. Contracts kept, the Crown's tax paid on time. That gets noticed.
if bridge.deaths_this_run > 0
	Royal Messenger: He knows you've buried some of your own. He says that's how you tell a guild from a sign over a door.
Royal Messenger: Villages past the border have gone quiet. No traders, no letters. The riders sent to look haven't come back.
Royal Messenger: His soldiers hold the roads. They can't go where your people go.
- Why us?
	Royal Messenger: Someone at court spoke your name. A Mages-Guild man, tall. I never got a look at his face.
- What does he want?
	Royal Messenger: Help. Not a favour. A commission, sealed and paid.
do bridge.choose("heard")
Royal Messenger: The King asks for your guild's help. He'll send for you soon. Have your best ready.
=> END
